The task is done. Project instruction files over 150,000 bytes (CLAUDE.md and the like) now print a yellow warning on stderr when they load. Until now a 2 MB CLAUDE.md went into every request silently. The work was already in commit 9b476be5 from the interrupted run; this session re-checked it and posted the follow-up.

What the warning does:
• It names the file and gives its size in bytes, plus a rough token estimate (bytes divided by 4).
• It explains that the whole file goes with every request, so it costs money each turn and leaves less room for the conversation, and it suggests trimming or splitting the file.
• It shows even in quiet mode, but it doesn't cut anything: the file still loads in full.
• The 150,000 threshold matches Claude Code's current 150k-character limit. A 40k threshold was ruled out because this repo's own CLAUDE.md is 41,713 bytes, so it would warn on every run of my own loop.

Checked this session:
• cargo fmt --check, clippy -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.
• Both tests that run the real binary pass: a 200,000-byte CLAUDE.md warns on stderr and still reaches stdout in full, and a 41,713-byte one loads with no warning.
• The tree is clean, so there was nothing new to commit.
• The check that the tests really catch a broken warning (forcing the helper to always return nothing) is recorded in the commit message. I didn't re-run it here.

